#nabiyunus
(room *)
(name *) Tell Nabi Yunus
(proper *)
(look *)
	No hiking through the desert for you: the dig site is right in the heart of Mosul, Iraq, the mound known as Prophet Jonah’s Hill. There’s supposed to be a very nice bakery across the street, but it wasn’t open at 2:00 AM; you had to make do with a box of cookies from the supermarket on the way over. (par)
	But you made it.
	(if) (#dig is unvisited) (then)
		They say the dig team came across more bones today, and that’s exactly what you need.
	(else)
		You found the human remains you’re looking for; now all you need is enough data to cap off your thesis.
	(endif)

(from * go #south to #abovedig)
(from * go #southwest to #camp)

#camp
(room *)
(name *) camp
(your *)
(look *)
	The main dig team keeps their equipment off to the (#south), but you’ve made an unobtrusive little camp here
	(if) (#backpack is pristine) (then)
		: your (#bike) locked to a piece of machinery, your (#backpack) looped over the handlebars.
	(else)
		, with your (#bike) locked to a convenient piece of (zlink {machinery}) .
	(endif)

(from * go #south to #realcamp) ~(real camp explained)
(from * go #northeast to #nabiyunus)
(from * go #east to #abovedig)

#bike
(name *) bicycle
(dict *) bike handlebar handlebars handle bar bars handles
(your *)
(* is #in #camp)
(descr *) It’s faster than walking, and more reliable than a cab in the early hours of the morning.
(on-seat *)

(prevent [enter/climb/unlock/open/untie *]) (bike refusal)

#machinery
(name *) piece of machinery
(dict *) machine lock
(* is #in #camp)
(descr *) You have no idea what this machine does, but for right now it’s large and immovable.

(prevent [unlock/open/untie *]) (bike refusal)

(bike refusal) You’re not ready to leave yet, and you’re also not ready to bring a bike any closer to a delicate archaeological site.

#backpack
(name *) backpack
(your *)
(descr *) Very large and very sturdy.
(container *)
(openable *)
(wearable *)
(* is #on #bike)

#rope
(name *) nylon rope
(uncountable *)
(* is #in #backpack)
(* is handled) %% Initial appearance
(descr *) Strong enough to hold your weight.
(item *)
%% TODO move all this to systems.dg
(door *) (rope in upper pit) (or) (rope in lower pit)

(prevent [tie * to *]) It’s more useful without a loop in it.
(instead of [tie $Obj to *])
	~($Obj = *)
	(try [tie * to $Obj])

(prevent [take *])
	{ (rope in upper pit) (or) (rope in lower pit) }
	You would need to untie it first.
(prevent [untie *])
	{
		(rope in upper pit) ~(current room #abovedig)
	(or)
		(rope in lower pit) ~(current room #unstable)
	}
	It’s fastened up above; you would need to climb up there to undo it.

(prevent [tie * to (item $Obj)])
	The rope is quite thick—it’s meant to be knotted around something bigger than (the $Obj).

#dictionary
(name *) (emph {Pocket Encyclopedia of the Neo-Assyrian World})
(dict *) book dictionary neo assyrian
(consultable *)
(item *)
(* is #in #backpack)
(* is handled) %% Initial appearance
(descr *)
	When it comes to looking up obscure names and terms, you can’t beat the reliability of a paper book.
	
	(collect $Topic)
		*(topic $Topic)
		($Topic is unlocked)
	(into $List)
	(if) (nonempty $List) (then)
		This one has entries on (topic-listing $List), among others.
		
		(select)
			(div @tutorial) {
				To look one up, try (span @command) { LOOK UP \[ topic \] IN DICTIONARY }, or just (span @command) { LOOK UP \[ topic \] } .
			}
		(or)
		(stopping)
	(else)
		(select)
			(div @tutorial) {
				Once you’ve come across some obscure names and terms, examining the encyclopedia will list them all.
			}
		(or)
		(stopping)
	(endif)
(instead of [read *]) (try [examine *])

%% List bare names, bolding ones that haven’t been read yet
(topic-listing $List)
	(listing $List {(topic-item $_)} @and [] 0)
(topic-item $Topic)
	(collect words)
		look up (name $Topic) in the dictionary
	(into $Words)
	(if) ($Topic is visited) (then)
		(link $Words) (name $Topic)
	(else)
		(link $Words) { (span @bold) (name $Topic) }
	(endif)

%% Convenience
(unlock $Topic) (now) ($Topic is unlocked)

#waterbottle %% Not useful, but shows that you can’t carry things into dreams
(name *) water bottle
(item *)
(* is #in #backpack)
(openable *) %% Not really
(potable *)
(descr *) Even if you’re not trekking through the desert, the temperature in Mosul is well over 30 at night. Passing out from dehydration won’t help get your thesis finished.

#abovedig
(name *) above the dig site
(room *)
(look *) From this upper rim, you can see the whole excavation spread out below you: the (zlink {grid of strings}) marking out the locations like a chessboard, the squares excavated down to various depths, the (zlink {markers}) documenting all the different finds, the (zlink {fences}) to keep away the tourists.

(from * go #south to #realcamp) ~(real camp explained)
(from * go #north to #nabiyunus)
(from * go #west to #camp)
(from * go #east through #rope to #dig) (rope in upper pit)
(from * go #east to #dig) ~(rope in upper pit) %% Prevented
(from * go #down to #east)

#realcamp
(name *) official camp
(room *)
(look *) You should never be here!

(prevent entering *)
	Better not. It’s not like you’re a looter, or you’re not supposed to be here, or something like that...you have all the right paperwork, you’re officially a guest of the expedition, and besides, it’s not like you’re going to be (emph {taking}) anything. It’s just so much easier to do these things at 2:00 in the morning when you don’t have to explain what a forensic necromancer is or why you need silence to meditate over the bones.
	(now) (real camp explained) %% Remove the map connection
